(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-2-herbal-medicines-medical-marijuana-quiz"]={"title":"Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana — Set 1 — PA Quizzes","category":"CAM / Nutrition","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What proportion of supplements sold online does the FDA estimate to be counterfeit?","choices":["About one in ten","About two out of every three","About one in three","About one in twenty"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture cites an FDA estimate that two out of every three supplements sold online is a counterfeit. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which pulmonary and vascular concerns does the lecture raise about cannabis?","choices":["It can worsen bronchitis and pneumonia, with a possible association with myocardial infarction, stroke and peripheral vascular disease","It improves pulmonary symptoms through bronchodilation","It has no cardiovascular associations at any dose","It causes pulmonary fibrosis in most long-term users"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists worsening pulmonary symptoms including bronchitis and pneumonia, and a possible association with myocardial infarction, stroke and peripheral vascular disease, adding that cannabis can exacerbate arrhythmia. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which form of coenzyme Q10 does the lecture describe as much better absorbed?","choices":["Ubiquinone, the oxidised form","Ubiquinol, the reduced form","Both are absorbed identically","Neither is absorbed orally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states ubiquinol, the reduced form, absorbs up to eight times better than ubiquinone and remains in the blood far longer. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What does first-pass metabolism of delta-9 THC produce, and how does that metabolite compare?","choices":["11-hydroxy-THC, which is longer lasting and more psychoactive than delta-9 THC","Cannabidiol, which is non-psychotropic and anti-inflammatory","11-hydroxy-THC, which is inactive and rapidly excreted in urine","A terpene metabolite responsible for the characteristic smell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states first-pass metabolism produces 11-hydroxy-THC, which is longer lasting and more psychoactive than delta-9 THC. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which herbal preparation does the lecture describe as containing alcohol?","choices":["Tea pills","Capsules","Tinctures","Teas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes tinctures as rapidly absorbed and containing alcohol. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which route does the lecture recommend for breakthrough symptoms, and in whom is it a poor choice?","choices":["Inhalation, and it is a poor choice in people with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or asthma triggered by inhalants","Oral, and it is a poor choice in people with malabsorption","Topical cream, and it is a poor choice in people with skin disease","Suppository, and it is a poor choice in people with constipation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture calls inhalation good for breakthrough symptoms and poor for people with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or asthma triggered by inhalants. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which populations does the lecture say require particular caution with herbal dosing?","choices":["Athletes and shift workers","Pregnancy and lactation, children, and the elderly with polypharmacy","Only patients over the age of eighty","Only patients taking more than five prescription drugs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture advises avoiding use in contemplated pregnancy and lactation, extreme care in dosing children with weight-based doses, and warns about polypharmacy in the elderly. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which herb interacts with cyclosporine, the drug used to prevent transplant rejection?","choices":["Lemon balm","Methylsulfonylmethane","Echinacea","Chamomile"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists cyclosporine and warfarin as chamomile interactions, describing cyclosporine as a drug used to prevent rejection of organ transplants. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Above roughly what single dose of THC does the lecture say euphoria can be produced?","choices":["Over 4 mg","Over 350 mg","Over 60 mg","Over 10 mg"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that over 10 mg of THC at one time can produce euphoria. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Why does the lecture warn against combining vitamin K, fish oil, and 'blood moving' herbs with warfarin?","choices":["They inactivate warfarin, causing clot formation and stroke","They increase the anticoagulant effect of warfarin, leading to bleeding","They cause warfarin to accumulate in fat stores and act unpredictably","They block warfarin absorption when taken at the same time of day"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states these can increase warfarin's anticoagulant effect and cause bleeding. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which contaminants does the lecture warn herbal products may contain?","choices":["Only bacterial and fungal organisms","Only pesticide residues from cultivation","Heavy metals, unlabeled prescription drugs, and toxic plants","Only excess vitamins from fortification"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists heavy metals, unlabeled prescription drugs and toxic plants as possible contaminants, since products are not regulated by the FDA for purity and potency. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How does the lecture define adaptogens?","choices":["Herbs that reliably raise blood pressure and heart rate under stress","Natural substances that help the body adapt to stress, support metabolic function, and restore balance","Plant compounds that act only on the central nervous system to induce sedation","Concentrated plant extracts standardized by the FDA for potency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines adaptogens this way and notes they restore endocrine balance, modulate immunity, and support homeostasis. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Why does stopping cannabis take a few weeks to relieve cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome?","choices":["Because cannabinoid receptors must be regenerated","Because antiemetics must first be withdrawn","Because hepatic enzyme induction persists for a month","Because of fat deposits"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that stopping cannabis consumption takes a few weeks due to fat deposits. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe cannabidiol's effect on the psychoactivity of THC?","choices":["It intensifies THC psychoactivity by increasing CB1 affinity","It has no effect on THC psychoactivity but treats seizures independently","It appears to reduce psychoactivity and other side effects of THC by altering CB1 shape","It blocks CB1 entirely, preventing any THC effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states cannabidiol appears to reduce psycho-activity and other side effects of THC by altering CB1 shape, and that taking cannabidiol with THC helps mitigate psychoactive effects. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slides 60 and 62"},{"q":"Under the 2023 Florida limits, what is the daily dose amount permitted for edibles?","choices":["190 mg THC","60 mg THC","195 mg THC","350 mg THC"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's dispensing table gives edibles a daily dose amount of 60 mg THC and a 70-day supply limit of 4,200 mg THC. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"According to the JAMA 2015 recommendations quoted in the lecture, an appropriate medical marijuana candidate should have which of the following?","choices":["A terminal diagnosis confirmed by a single physician","Any chronic condition, provided the patient requests a certification","A debilitating condition with supportive randomised trial data, multiple failed first- and second-line trials, and failed use of an FDA approved cannabinoid","Failure of one first-line agent and no other requirement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these together with no active substance abuse, psychotic disorder, or unstable mood or anxiety disorder. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Where are CB1 receptors predominantly found, and what does that explain?","choices":["Mainly in the immune system, explaining the anti-inflammatory effects","Mainly in hepatic tissue, explaining first-pass metabolism","Mainly in the gastrointestinal tract, explaining the antiemetic effects","Mainly in the central nervous system and throughout the brain, explaining the psychoactive effects of THC"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture places CB1 receptors in the central nervous system and throughout the brain, which accounts for THC's psychoactive effects, and also notes immune, gastrointestinal, reproductive, adrenal, heart, lung and bladder tissue. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which reasons does the lecture give for patients not telling their provider they use herbs?","choices":["They consider supplement use too expensive to admit to","They believe supplements are already listed in the medical record","They are told by manufacturers not to disclose supplement use","They assume natural products are harmless and fear the provider will be skeptical"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives both the assumption that natural products are harmless and fear that providers may be skeptical of their use. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"With which drug classes does the lecture say interactions should be expected?","choices":["Only anticoagulants and antiplatelet agents","Only drugs metabolised by cytochrome P-450 2D6","Drugs with sympathomimetic activity, central nervous system depressants, and drugs with anticholinergic effects","No interactions are expected because cannabis is not hepatically metabolised"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names these classes and gives alcohol, benzodiazepines, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors and zolpidem as examples, noting few data exist and predictions rest on cytochrome P-450. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which herb is described as stimulating bile flow and used by herbalists for fatty liver and cirrhosis, and as a good source of iron, potassium and zinc?","choices":["Dandelion","Chicory","Milk thistle","Burdock"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes dandelion as stimulating bile flow from the liver, used against fatty liver, cirrhosis, estrogen dominance and acne, and a good source of iron, potassium and zinc. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"For which indications did the 2015 meta-analysis cited in the lecture include randomised controlled trials?","choices":["Only chronic pain and spasticity due to multiple sclerosis","Nausea and vomiting with chemotherapy, appetite stimulation in HIV/AIDS, chronic pain, spasticity in multiple sclerosis, depression, anxiety, sleep disorder, and glaucoma","Only psychiatric indications such as depression, anxiety, and sleep disorder","Only conditions on the Florida automatic qualifying list"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists all of these indications for the 2015 meta-analysis and notes most studies showed only modest symptom improvements. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"In what month and year was the Florida medical marijuana law passed, according to the lecture?","choices":["November 2016","January 2014","December 2020","June 2023"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the law passed in Florida in November 2016. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which three amino acids combine to form glutathione, as given in the lecture?","choices":["Cysteine, glycine, and glutamine","Methionine, lysine, and arginine","Taurine, serine, and alanine","Tryptophan, tyrosine, and valine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes glutathione as a combination of cysteine, glycine and glutamine, with its detoxifying power in the sulfur. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which caution does the lecture attach to milk thistle?","choices":["It may raise blood pressure, so avoid it with antihypertensives","It may cause allergic skin reactions when used topically","It may cause photosensitivity, so patients should limit sun exposure","It may lower blood sugar, so patients with diabetes or hypoglycemia should use caution"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture warns that milk thistle may lower blood sugar and that people with diabetes or hypoglycemia, or those on drugs affecting blood sugar, should use caution. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What does the lecture require for a terminal condition to qualify a patient in Florida?","choices":["Certification by the same physician who issues the recommendation","A hospice referral within the previous 30 days","Diagnosis by a physician other than the qualified physician issuing the certification","Confirmation by the Florida Department of Health"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The qualifying list includes a terminal condition diagnosed by a physician other than the qualified physician issuing the physician certification, documented by a second physician. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slides 56 and 69"},{"q":"What does the lecture say flaxseed does to the absorption of other medications?","choices":["It can slow the absorption of all medications","It speeds the absorption of all medications","It has no effect on the absorption of other medications","It blocks absorption of fat-soluble vitamins only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states flaxseed can slow the absorption of all medications, and lists interactions with anticoagulants, diabetes medications, contraceptives, hormones and antihyperlipidaemic agents. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"How does the lecture characterize the usual cause of prescriber ignorance about herbal supplements?","choices":["A genuine absence of any published data on herbal products","A requirement that only pharmacists counsel on supplements","Restrictions that prevent clinicians from discussing supplements","A practitioner's reluctance or disapproval about seeking out information for patients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says prescriber ignorance often is not caused by lack of reliable data but rather by reluctance or disapproval about seeking out information. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What proportion of the world's population does the World Health Organization estimate relies on herbal medicine?","choices":["About 80 percent","About 60 percent","About 40 percent","About 95 percent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture cites a World Health Organization estimate from 2006 that 80 percent of the world's population relies on herbal medicine. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which form of licorice does the lecture name for canker sores, peptic ulcers and reflux?","choices":["Whole licorice root taken as a tea","Licorice combined with bromelain","Deglycyrrhizinated licorice","Licorice extract standardised to glycyrrhizin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names deglycyrrhizinated licorice for canker sores, peptic ulcers and gastro-oesophageal reflux disease, alongside traditional use for cough, asthma and sore throat. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about cannabis use in young adults and psychosis?","choices":["Studies show it prevents psychosis in high risk individuals","Studies suggest it increases the risk of developing psychosis in high risk individuals","No association has been examined in young adults","The risk applies only to products high in cannabidiol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes studies suggest cannabis use in young adults increases the risk of developing psychosis in high risk individuals. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 62"}]};
